What tilt is acceptable? A face-runout share of 0.02 mm peak (a tenth of the 0.30 mm TIR window [repo]) allows tilt = 0.0185 degrees;
use quotes 0.05 degree = 0.054 mm peak, 0.11 mm peak to peak: 0.054 mm peak.
A 3-ball top seat with 10 um scatter at 150 mm span leaves 0.0038 degrees, i.e. 4.1 um peak: about 5 times inside that share. A lab jack scissor (0.3 degrees, illustrative, sourcing 6) is 0.32 mm peak.

The fine axes trim a tilt's position but not the tilt: the once-per-turn vertical term stays. Peak to peak at the seam for 0.02 degree: 0.043 mm.

Escape as a tier drop (the work leaves the head), beam vertical component 0.712 in the kit's opening pose [derived]:
  10 mm along the beam = 14.0 mm of Z
  20 mm along the beam = 28.1 mm of Z
  30 mm along the beam = 42.1 mm of Z
  40 mm along the beam = 56.2 mm of Z
  at 60 mm/s the drop of 28.1 mm takes 0.47 s, and the seam has turned 3.7 mm at 8 mm/s while it happens (0.47 s x 8 mm/s = 3.7 mm).
  the fine Z alone (12 mm of range) gives at most 8.5 mm along the beam.

Swap clearance with a drop of D: the rim clears the barrel by (5.0 mm tip margin + D - fine Z high stop) before the shuttle starts. With D = 40 and Z at +6:  39 mm; without the tier and Z at +6 the barrel is 4 mm into the rim (use-13: red from Z = +2 in travel-01's own scene).
